# ~/.profile -- read once per login session, not per shell.
#
# Who reads this file, and how it gets here:
#
#   login shell: console, `bash -l`, `ssh host`, Terminal.app on macOS
#       /etc/profile (which sources /etc/bash.bashrc) -> then the FIRST of
#       ~/.bash_profile, ~/.bash_login, ~/.profile. This file is read only
#       because neither of the other two exists. Creating either one shadows
#       this file completely and silently -- no error, the PATH additions below
#       simply stop happening.
#
#   the X11 desktop session, and it reaches everything the desktop runs
#       /etc/gdm3/Xsession is a #!/bin/sh script, and at its line 91 it does
#       `test -f "$HOME/.profile" && . "$HOME/.profile"`. So ~/.sh_env below
#       lands in the Xsession shell's environment.
#       Xsession.d/99x11-common_start then execs gnome-session as a child of
#       that shell, so gnome-shell inherits it by plain fork/exec; and
#       gnome-session-binary calls UnsetAndSetEnvironment on
#       org.freedesktop.systemd1.Manager, pushing the whole environment into
#       `systemd --user`. Every user unit and every DBus-activated or
#       .desktop-launched app inherits it from there.
#
#       So treat .sh_env as session-wide rather than shell-local. The private
#       ~/.global_vars is deliberately NOT sourced from it for this reason: it
#       holds API tokens, and is read from the interactive halves of .bashrc
#       and .zshrc instead, which this route never reaches.
#
#       Under Wayland the route genuinely does not exist: GDM starts the
#       session through systemd --user units and never runs Xsession, so this
#       file is not read at all. That is the case environment.d was added for.
#       This machine is X11 on purpose (WaylandEnable=false, see readme.txt),
#       so here the route is live.
#
#   a login `sh` (dash on Debian)
#       also reads it, which is why nothing here may use bashisms. Keep it
#       POSIX: `[ ]` not `[[ ]]`, no arrays, no `source`.
#
# Who does NOT read this file:
#
#   new terminal tab, `bash`, `su`     ~/.bashrc only.
#   `ssh host 'some command'`          ~/.bashrc only -- bash reads it there
#                                      because stdin is a socket.
#   `bash script.sh`, `bash -c ...`    only $BASH_ENV, if set.
#   `systemd --user`                   started by pam_systemd at login, before
#                                      this file runs, so it begins with a
#                                      clean environment. It does not stay
#                                      clean on X11 -- gnome-session imports
#                                      the Xsession environment into it, see
#                                      above. ~/.config/environment.d/ is the
#                                      route that is meant to feed it.
#
# Note it sources ~/.bashrc itself, below, so a login bash ends up running both.
#
# see /usr/share/doc/bash/examples/startup-files for examples.
# the files are located in the bash-doc package.

# the default umask is set in /etc/profile; for setting the umask
# for ssh logins, install and configure the libpam-umask package.
#umask 022

# The shared environment -- PATH, CONFPATH, locale, editor, toolchains. First,
# because a login `sh`/dash reads neither .zshenv nor .bashrc and this is its
# only route to any of it. In bash it is also a no-op repeat of what .bashrc
# does below: .sh_env guards itself with an exported SH_ENV_LOADED, so the
# second read does nothing.
if [ -f "$HOME/.sh_env" ]; then
    . "$HOME/.sh_env"
fi

# if running bash
if [ -n "$BASH_VERSION" ]; then
    # include .bashrc if it exists
    if [ -f "$HOME/.bashrc" ]; then
	. "$HOME/.bashrc"
    fi
fi
